research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-559-- **The two exponents are what the construction turns on.** Differentiability at $0$ needs $c_n/\alpha_n \to 0$, and unboundedness of $G'$ needs $c_n/h_n \to \infty$; with $\alpha_n = 1/\iota(n+2)$ the choices $c_n = \alpha_n^{2}$ and $h_n = \alpha_n^{4}$ give $c_n/\alpha_n = \alpha_n \to 0$ and $c_n/h_n = \alpha_n^{-2} \to \infty$. Any pair of exponents with the same two properties would do; these are verified explicitly in steps 6.1 and 6.2 because the construction is only as good as those two inequalities.
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-560-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md:561:- **What this refutes and what it does not.** It refutes the claim that every derivative is Riemann integrable, hence the naive reading of [[thm-ftc-second-part]] with its integrability hypothesis deleted. It says nothing about whether $G'$ has a primitive — it does, namely $G$ — and nothing about the sharp class of functions for which $\int_a^b G' = G(b)-G(a)$ holds, which this library records but does not prove ([[rem-integral-conventions-and-scope]]).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-562-````
