research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-1424-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-1425-**Which page it serves.** This is the natural endpoint of the fundamental
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-1426-theorems of calculus page, and the reason that page's results are called the
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md:1427:working FTC rather than the FTC. That page proves: if $f$ is Riemann integrable
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-1428-on $[a,b]$ and $F$ is any antiderivative of $f$ on $[a,b]$, then
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-1429-$\int_a^b f = F(b) - F(a)$; and if $f$ is continuous then $x \mapsto \int_a^x f$
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-1430-is an antiderivative. Both statements carry hypotheses that the theorem above
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-1431-deletes. The library states the sharp version here so that no reader concludes
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md:1432:the working FTC is the last word, and so that the counterexamples on that page
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-1433-(a derivative that is not Riemann integrable, the Cantor function, Volterra's
research/audit/wave11-refuter-retry-tasks/a6r2-rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-1434-function) have a stated theorem to be counterexamples to.
