research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-substitution.result.json:16:  "tail": "VERDICT: CLEAN\n\nBOUNDARY: Checked φ constant (c<d, m=M gives degenerate [m,M]), φ(c)=φ(d) (both sides zero via oriented integral), φ(c)>φ(d) (orientation flip via def-oriented-integral and primitive), φ(c)<φ(d) (direct L3 evaluation). Nondegenerate order-convex J with at least two points ensures every point is a limit point, satisfying the chain‑rule hypothesis. No hidden monotonicity or injectivity is required; the proof holds for all differentiable φ with integrable φ′.\n\nSOURCES: Inspected all 15 declared dependencies exactly as supplied: cor‑primitives‑of‑a‑continuous‑function, thm‑ftc‑second‑part, thm‑chain‑rule, thm‑composition‑with‑a‑continuous‑function, cor‑integrability‑of‑absolute‑values‑products‑and‑lattice‑operations, thm‑continuous‑implies‑integrable, cor‑continuous‑image‑of‑an‑intereval‑is‑an‑intereval, def‑oriented‑integral, def‑derivative, def‑continuity‑real, cor‑differentiable‑implies‑continuous, def‑intereval, def‑limit‑point‑r, def‑darboux‑integral, def‑max‑min. Each statement’s hypotheses, quantifiers, and conclusions match the usage in the proof steps; no reversal, missing condition, or boundary mismatch was found.\n\nCHECKED: Confirmed title, the complete public claim, every numbered step (1.1‑6.1), and the Remarks. The inference chain is valid: φ continuous⇒integrable (L1); primitive F exists (L3); image is a closed interval (L2) and lies in J; the chain rule (L4) gives differentiability of F∘φ; continuity of f on [m,M] plus integrability of φ yield integrability of f∘φ (L5); product with φ′ is integrable (L6); the second FTC (L7) evaluates ∫(f∘φ)φ′; the oriented integral makes the left side equal to the same primitive increment. No circularity, hidden division, or false existential choice is present."
