research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-5926-research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-page-properties-of-the-integral-and-the-working-ftc.log:7082:library/real-analysis/darboux-lhopital-and-taylor.md:27:The derivative and mean value theorems supply Rolle's theorem, Cauchy's mean value theorem, and the algebra of derivatives. Finite counting supplies binomial coefficients and Pascal's rule. Together these prerequisites support repeated differentiation, quotient comparisons, and finite Taylor polynomials without presupposing power-series theory.
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-5927-research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-page-properties-of-the-integral-and-the-working-ftc.log:7083:library/real-analysis/darboux-lhopital-and-taylor.md:29:Higher derivatives and smoothness lead to the general Leibniz rule and higher-order Rolle arguments. Darboux's theorem then controls the intermediate values of derivatives, while the two L'Hôpital theorems treat the zero-over-zero and infinity-over-infinity forms. Taylor polynomials culminate in the Schlömilch-Roche, Lagrange, Cauchy, and Peano remainders, followed by remainder bounds and derivative tests for local extrema.
