research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105360-+{"id":"def-the-integral-function","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources define the variable-upper-limit integral function F(x)=integral_a^x f. The item merely fixes the same object in the library's oriented-integral notation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105361-+{"id":"thm-the-integral-function-is-lipschitz","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The source proves that the integral function of a bounded Riemann-integrable function satisfies |F(x)-F(y)|<=M|x-y| and is continuous. The item's proof is the same mathematical estimate but was written locally from its integral bounds.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105362-+{"id":"thm-ftc-first-part","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources state that the integral function of a Riemann-integrable f is differentiable at every point where f is continuous, with derivative f at that point. The repository adds explicit one-sided endpoint language already fixed by its derivative definition.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1105363:+{"id":"thm-ftc-second-part","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources state the Newton-Leibniz formula for a differentiable function whose derivative is Riemann integrable, with the same conclusion. The item's proof through the first FTC and constant-difference theorem is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105364-+{"id":"cor-primitives-of-a-continuous-function","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources give existence of the integral-function primitive, uniqueness up to an additive constant, and evaluation of definite integrals by any primitive. The item collects these standard consequences in one corollary and applies its endpoint convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105365-+{"id":"thm-integration-by-parts","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources state integration by parts for continuously differentiable functions. The item uses the sharper Riemann hypothesis that u and v are differentiable and u' and v' are integrable, then derives integrability of the products; the identity and direction are unchanged.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105366-+{"id":"thm-substitution","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources give change of variables for continuous f and a C^1 substitution. The item weakens C^1 to differentiability with Riemann-integrable derivative and uses the same oriented identity. Its proof through a primitive and the chain rule is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105367-+{"id":"thm-second-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Lebesgue_integral"],"rationale":"The source states Bonnet's second mean value formula for monotone g and integrable f, with the same endpoint coefficients and an intermediate xi, in the stronger Lebesgue setting. The item specializes it to bounded Riemann-integrable f and its oriented convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105368-+{"id":"thm-nonnegative-continuous-with-zero-integral-vanishes","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/~rbm/18.100-F02.HMW/HMW7s.pdf","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_rintprop.html"],"rationale":"The sources state that a nonnegative continuous function on a compact interval with zero integral is identically zero. The item's positive-neighbourhood lower-bound proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105369-+{"id":"thm-integral-test-for-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_impropriemann.html"],"rationale":"The source states the integral test for a positive decreasing function and its sampled series. The item reformulates convergence of the improper integral as boundedness of the proper-integral sequence indexed from zero, allows nonincreasing nonnegative f, and records explicit comparison inequalities.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1105370:+{"id":"rem-integral-conventions-and-scope","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"none","urls":[],"rationale":"This is a repository-specific convention and scope record, including reading-order statements and deliberate omissions. Its mathematical descriptions and every linked target were checked on disk; the stale Taylor-integral-remainder plan sentence is recorded separately as published-claim decay.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1105371:+{"id":"ex-polynomial-integrals-by-the-ftc","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~demmel/ma128a_Spr02/LectureNotes/Integration.html"],"rationale":"The university notes state exactly integral_0^1 x^m dx=1/(m+1) for nonnegative integer m. The item translates the integer into the library's canonical real embedding and supplies local FTC and consistency checks.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105372-+{"id":"ex-additivity-on-a-step-function","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"The particular two-level step function and its paired verification routes are a local worked construction. Splitting at 1 gives two constant integrals 1 and 3, and the displayed three-point Darboux partition gives matching sums after the single jump is isolated; the value 4 is directly verified.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105373-+{"id":"ex-integral-test-applied-to-the-p-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_series.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_impropriemann.html"],"rationale":"The sources give the p-series threshold p>1 and the integral test. The item specializes to rational powers available in the library, indexes f_p(t)=(t+1)^{-p} from zero, and expresses improper convergence as boundedness of its proper-integral sequence. The mathematical threshold is sourced, but its last p=1 sentence falsely denies existence of a primitive and is proposed for repair.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1105374:+{"id":"ex-two-root-x-and-its-unbounded-derivative","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www2.hawaii.edu/~gradysw/apex/sec_derivative.html","https://faculty.gvsu.edu/boelkinm/Home/ACS/sec-6-5-improper.html"],"rationale":"The sources state that sqrt(x) is continuous, has derivative 1/(2sqrt(x)) for x>0, is not differentiable at zero, and has an unbounded derivative near zero. The item scales by 2 and packages those facts to explain why the proper Riemann FTC does not apply on [0,1].","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105375-+{"id":"cex-an-integrable-function-with-no-primitive","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the sign function's integral function as an absolute-value function and Darboux's theorem that derivatives cannot jump. The item packages these standard facts as an explicit refutation of the claim that every Riemann-integrable function has a primitive.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105376-+{"id":"cex-a-function-with-a-primitive-that-is-not-integrable","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"none","urls":[],"rationale":"The exact disjoint-interval quartic bump construction is a bespoke polynomial replacement for the classical trigonometric witness. The proof checks seam derivatives, differentiability at the accumulation point, and the displayed unbounded derivative values directly. No semantically identical source for this construction surfaced, and its deliberate coefficient and interval design supports the positive local-invention determination.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1105377-+{"id":"cex-a-composition-of-integrable-functions-that-is-not-integrable","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00591-022-00318-x"],"rationale":"The peer-reviewed source gives the same counterexample: Thomae's Riemann-integrable function composed with the integrable indicator of the positive range becomes Dirichlet's nonintegrable function. The repository's discontinuity-set verification is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
