research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1133843-+{"id":"thm-ftc-first-part","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources state that the integral function of a Riemann-integrable f is differentiable at every point where f is continuous, with derivative f at that point. The repository adds explicit one-sided endpoint language already fixed by its derivative definition.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1133844:+{"id":"thm-ftc-second-part","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources state the Newton-Leibniz formula for a differentiable function whose derivative is Riemann integrable, with the same conclusion. The item's proof through the first FTC and constant-difference theorem is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1133845-+{"id":"cor-primitives-of-a-continuous-function","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources give existence of the integral-function primitive, uniqueness up to an additive constant, and evaluation of definite integrals by any primitive. The item collects these standard consequences in one corollary and applies its endpoint convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1133846-+{"id":"thm-integration-by-parts","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources state integration by parts for continuously differentiable functions. The item uses the sharper Riemann hypothesis that u and v are differentiable and u' and v' are integrable, then derives integrability of the products; the identity and direction are unchanged.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1133847-+{"id":"thm-substitution","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources give change of variables for continuous f and a C^1 substitution. The item weakens C^1 to differentiability with Riemann-integrable derivative and uses the same oriented identity. Its proof through a primitive and the chain rule is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
