research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1319440-+{"id":"thm-ftc-first-part","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources state that the integral function of a Riemann-integrable f is differentiable at every point where f is continuous, with derivative f at that point. The repository adds explicit one-sided endpoint language already fixed by its derivative definition.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1319441:+{"id":"thm-ftc-second-part","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources state the Newton-Leibniz formula for a differentiable function whose derivative is Riemann integrable, with the same conclusion. The item's proof through the first FTC and constant-difference theorem is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1319442-+{"id":"cor-primitives-of-a-continuous-function","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources give existence of the integral-function primitive, uniqueness up to an additive constant, and evaluation of definite integrals by any primitive. The item collects these standard consequences in one corollary and applies its endpoint convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1319443-+{"id":"thm-integration-by-parts","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources state integration by parts for continuously differentiable functions. The item uses the sharper Riemann hypothesis that u and v are differentiable and u' and v' are integrable, then derives integrability of the products; the identity and direction are unchanged.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1319444-+{"id":"thm-substitution","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html"],"rationale":"The sources give change of variables for continuous f and a C^1 substitution. The item weakens C^1 to differentiability with Riemann-integrable derivative and uses the same oriented identity. Its proof through a primitive and the chain rule is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1319445-+{"id":"thm-second-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Lebesgue_integral"],"rationale":"The source states Bonnet's second mean value formula for monotone g and integrable f, with the same endpoint coefficients and an intermediate xi, in the stronger Lebesgue setting. The item specializes it to bounded Riemann-integrable f and its oriented convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1319446-+{"id":"thm-nonnegative-continuous-with-zero-integral-vanishes","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/~rbm/18.100-F02.HMW/HMW7s.pdf","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_rintprop.html"],"rationale":"The sources state that a nonnegative continuous function on a compact interval with zero integral is identically zero. The item's positive-neighbourhood lower-bound proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1319447-+{"id":"thm-integral-test-for-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_impropriemann.html"],"rationale":"The source states the integral test for a positive decreasing function and its sampled series. The item reformulates convergence of the improper integral as boundedness of the proper-integral sequence indexed from zero, allows nonincreasing nonnegative f, and records explicit comparison inequalities.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
