research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1319449:+{"id":"ex-polynomial-integrals-by-the-ftc","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~demmel/ma128a_Spr02/LectureNotes/Integration.html"],"rationale":"The university notes state exactly integral_0^1 x^m dx=1/(m+1) for nonnegative integer m. The item translates the integer into the library's canonical real embedding and supplies local FTC and consistency checks.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1319450-+{"id":"ex-additivity-on-a-step-function","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"The particular two-level step function and its paired verification routes are a local worked construction. Splitting at 1 gives two constant integrals 1 and 3, and the displayed three-point Darboux partition gives matching sums after the single jump is isolated; the value 4 is directly verified.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1319451-+{"id":"ex-integral-test-applied-to-the-p-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_series.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_impropriemann.html"],"rationale":"The sources give the p-series threshold p>1 and the integral test. The item specializes to rational powers available in the library, indexes f_p(t)=(t+1)^{-p} from zero, and expresses improper convergence as boundedness of its proper-integral sequence. The mathematical threshold is sourced, but its last p=1 sentence falsely denies existence of a primitive and is proposed for repair.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1319452:+{"id":"ex-two-root-x-and-its-unbounded-derivative","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www2.hawaii.edu/~gradysw/apex/sec_derivative.html","https://faculty.gvsu.edu/boelkinm/Home/ACS/sec-6-5-improper.html"],"rationale":"The sources state that sqrt(x) is continuous, has derivative 1/(2sqrt(x)) for x>0, is not differentiable at zero, and has an unbounded derivative near zero. The item scales by 2 and packages those facts to explain why the proper Riemann FTC does not apply on [0,1].","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1319453-+{"id":"cex-an-integrable-function-with-no-primitive","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the sign function's integral function as an absolute-value function and Darboux's theorem that derivatives cannot jump. The item packages these standard facts as an explicit refutation of the claim that every Riemann-integrable function has a primitive.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1319454-+{"id":"cex-a-function-with-a-primitive-that-is-not-integrable","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"none","urls":[],"rationale":"The exact disjoint-interval quartic bump construction is a bespoke polynomial replacement for the classical trigonometric witness. The proof checks seam derivatives, differentiability at the accumulation point, and the displayed unbounded derivative values directly. No semantically identical source for this construction surfaced, and its deliberate coefficient and interval design supports the positive local-invention determination.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1319455-+{"id":"cex-a-composition-of-integrable-functions-that-is-not-integrable","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00591-022-00318-x"],"rationale":"The peer-reviewed source gives the same counterexample: Thomae's Riemann-integrable function composed with the integrable indicator of the positive range becomes Dirichlet's nonintegrable function. The repository's discontinuity-set verification is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
