research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1448403-+{"id":"ex-integral-test-applied-to-the-p-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_series.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_impropriemann.html"],"rationale":"The sources give the p-series threshold p>1 and the integral test. The item specializes to rational powers available in the library, indexes f_p(t)=(t+1)^{-p} from zero, and expresses improper convergence as boundedness of its proper-integral sequence. The mathematical threshold is sourced, but its last p=1 sentence falsely denies existence of a primitive and is proposed for repair.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1448404:+{"id":"ex-two-root-x-and-its-unbounded-derivative","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www2.hawaii.edu/~gradysw/apex/sec_derivative.html","https://faculty.gvsu.edu/boelkinm/Home/ACS/sec-6-5-improper.html"],"rationale":"The sources state that sqrt(x) is continuous, has derivative 1/(2sqrt(x)) for x>0, is not differentiable at zero, and has an unbounded derivative near zero. The item scales by 2 and packages those facts to explain why the proper Riemann FTC does not apply on [0,1].","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1448405-+{"id":"cex-an-integrable-function-with-no-primitive","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the sign function's integral function as an absolute-value function and Darboux's theorem that derivatives cannot jump. The item packages these standard facts as an explicit refutation of the claim that every Riemann-integrable function has a primitive.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
