items/cex-open-cover-with-no-lebesgue-number.md:62:[L3] A Lebesgue number for a cover is a real $\delta > 0$ such that every nonempty subset of diameter less than $\delta$ lies inside a single member of the cover; a compact metric space has one for every open cover ([[thm-lebesgue-number-lemma]]).
items/cex-open-cover-with-no-lebesgue-number.md:76:4.1 So no real $\delta > 0$ is a Lebesgue number for this cover, and the claim [A1] is refuted; since $(0,1)$ is not compact, the compactness hypothesis of the Lebesgue number lemma is not removable. [A1, L1, L3, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎
items/cex-open-cover-with-no-lebesgue-number.md:80:**What goes wrong.** The members of the cover grow towards $(0,1)$ but none of them reaches down to $0$, so a set clinging to $0$ of any positive diameter is never captured whole. On a compact space the finitely many members of a subcover put a uniform floor under this, and that floor is the Lebesgue number ([[thm-lebesgue-number-lemma]]).
