items/ex-integral-test-applied-to-the-p-series.md:122:- **The index shift is real and is checked in step 2.2.** The $p$-series starts at $k = 1$ because $0^{p} = 0$ has no reciprocal, while a sequence in this library is a function on $\mathbb{N}$, which contains $0$; the integrand $(t+1)^{-p}$ is shifted by exactly one for that reason, and $f_p(k) = 1/\iota(k+1)^{p}$. Substituting $t^{-p}$ for $(t+1)^{-p}$ would put an undefined value at $t = 0$ and make $\int_0^N$ improper.
